# Live RevolutionPlot

This notebook is mostly educational and shows how to work with updates on a low-level. To achieve the same goal you can always put `RevolutionPlot` inside `Manipulate` expression.

<br/>

## Decomposing native plot function

Using the output of the standard plotting functions we craft a dynamic version of it. For example

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
# Live RevolutionPlot

This notebook is mostly educational and shows how to work with updates on a low-level. To achieve the same goal you can always put `RevolutionPlot` inside `Manipulate` expression.

<br />

## Decomposing native plot function

Using the output of the standard plotting functions we craft a dynamic version of it. For example

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  InitGroup: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
model = RevolutionPlot3D[{Sin[t] + Sin[5 t] /10,
   Cos[t] + Cos[5 t] /10}, {t, 0, Pi},
  RegionFunction -> (Sin[5 (#4 + #5)] > 0 &), Mesh -> None,
  BoundaryStyle -> Black, PlotStyle -> Thickness[.1], MaxRecursion->1]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(FrontEndRef["bf7df6e9-a409-4146-9f64-bb8be5a440f1"])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRCeEJBwK8rPK3HNS3GtSE0uLUlMykkNVgEKJ6WZp6SZpVrqJpoYWOqaGJqY6VqmmZnoJiVZJKWaJpqYGKQZAgCNJBXe"*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Here we can find, that no matter how we change the parmeters of the curve our graphics is packed into a single `GraphicsComplex`

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Here we can find, that no matter how we change the parmeters of the curve our graphics is packed into a single `GraphicsComplex`

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Cases[model, _GraphicsComplex, 3] // First // Short 

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
GraphicsComplex[{{3.639180084066151`*^-7,1.6346410098583916`*^-13,1.1499999999999044`},{0.3372026534904982`,1.5143515717752036`*^-7,1.0618546140245668`},<<7121>>},<<1>>]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
